The default and the right answer for perhaps 90% of indexes: any type with a total order, equality and range predicates, sorting, uniqueness.
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_placed_at ON orders (placed_at);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_placed_at_desc ON orders (placed_at DESC NULLS LAST);

Equality only. WAL-logged and crash-safe since PostgreSQL 10 (before that they were not replicated and had to be rebuilt after a crash, which is why old advice says "never use hash indexes").
CREATE INDEX idx_sessions_token ON sessions USING hash (token);
Narrower than a B-tree for long keys, since it stores a 32-bit hash rather than the value. In exchange it cannot do ranges, cannot sort, cannot be unique, and cannot be multicolumn. The niche is genuinely narrow: very long keys, equality-only access, no ordering requirement. Benchmark against a B-tree on your own data before committing.

For naturally hierarchical, non-balanced partitionings: quadtrees, k-d trees, radix trees. In practice: inet/cidr containment, points, and text prefix search where a radix tree beats a B-tree on space.
CREATE INDEX idx_logs_ip ON logs USING spgist (client_ip inet_ops);
CREATE INDEX idx_words_radix ON words USING spgist (word);

Stores a summary (by default min/max) for each range of heap pages — 128 pages by default. Consequently it is minuscule: an index over a billion-row table can be a few megabytes.
CREATE INDEX idx_events_at_brin ON events USING brin (occurred_at);
CREATE INDEX idx_events_at_brin2 ON events USING brin (occurred_at) WITH (pages_per_range = 32);
BRIN only works when the physical row order correlates with the indexed value — append-only time-series data, log tables, tables loaded in key order. If rows are scattered, every block range spans the whole value domain and the index eliminates nothing. Check before building:
SELECT attname, correlation FROM pg_stats
WHERE tablename = 'events' AND attname = 'occurred_at';
-- |correlation| near 1.0 → BRIN is viable. Near 0 → useless.
PostgreSQL 14 added minmax_multi opclasses (tolerant of a few outliers that would otherwise ruin a range's summary) and bloom opclasses (equality on uncorrelated columns).

EXPLAIN SELECT …; -- plan only, does not execute
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT …; -- executes and reports real timings and I/O
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, VERBOSE, SETTINGS, FORMAT TEXT) SELECT …;
PostgreSQL 18 includes BUFFERS by default whenever ANALYZE is used, so EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) alone gives the I/O picture on 18; on 17 and earlier you must ask for it. SETTINGS reports any planner GUC that differs from its default — the first thing to check when a plan differs between two environments. track_io_timing = on (Post 00) adds real read/write times to the buffer lines.
Watch out: EXPLAIN ANALYZE runs the statement. For UPDATE/DELETE/INSERT, wrap it: BEGIN; EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) DELETE …; ROLLBACK;

of MongoDB's hideIndex() — the "hide it for a week and watch" pattern from the MongoDB course does not translate directly. A UPDATE pg_index SET indisvalid = false hack is widely circulated for this purpose; avoid it, because direct catalog manipulation is unsupported and interacts badly with cached plans. The honest procedure instead:
-- 1. record the definition so recreating it is trivial
SELECT pg_get_indexdef('idx_maybe_unused'::regclass);
-- 2. drop it online
DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_maybe_unused;
-- 3. monitor. If anything regresses, recreate it — also online:
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_maybe_unused ON …;
Because both the drop and the recreate are concurrent operations, the rollback costs nothing but build time. That makes dropping a suspect index a low-risk experiment rather than a commitment.

Create a one-million-row orders table with generate_series, ANALYZE it, and time SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 before and after adding an index.
Solution
CREATE TABLE orders (
id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
customer_id int NOT NULL,
status text NOT NULL,
total numeric(10,2) NOT NULL,
placed_at timestamptz NOT NULL
);
INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, status, total, placed_at)
SELECT (random() * 50000)::int + 1,
(ARRAY['pending','paid','shipped','cancelled'])[(random()*3)::int + 1],
(random() * 500)::numeric(10,2),
now() - (random() * 365) * interval '1 day'
FROM generate_series(1, 1000000);
ANALYZE orders;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);
ANALYZE orders;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;
Expect Seq Scan → Bitmap Heap Scan or Index Scan, with Buffers: read dropping by orders of magnitude.

Add an index that makes SELECT customer_id, placed_at FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 an Index Only Scan with Heap Fetches: 0. Then UPDATE a thousand rows and re-run without vacuuming. Explain what changed.
Solution
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_cust_placed ON orders (customer_id, placed_at);
VACUUM ANALYZE orders;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT customer_id, placed_at FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;
-- Index Only Scan … Heap Fetches: 0
UPDATE orders SET total = total + 1 WHERE customer_id = 42;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT customer_id, placed_at FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;
-- Heap Fetches > 0: those pages are no longer all-visible until VACUUM runs

Make WHERE lower(email) = lower($1) use an index. Verify with EXPLAIN, then show that a plain index on email does not help.
Solution
CREATE INDEX idx_users_lower_email ON users (lower(email));
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE lower(email) = lower('ADA@example.com');
-- Index Scan using idx_users_lower_email
DROP INDEX idx_users_lower_email;
CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users (email);
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE lower(email) = lower('ADA@example.com');
-- Seq Scan: lower(email) is a different value from email

Given that fewer than 1% of orders are pending, build a partial index for the open-orders queue and compare its size against a full index on the same column.
Solution
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_full ON orders (placed_at);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_pending ON orders (placed_at) WHERE status = 'pending';
SELECT indexrelname, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid))
FROM pg_stat_user_indexes WHERE relname = 'orders';
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY placed_at LIMIT 50;
